    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5194/gmd-8-2221-2015",
    "abstract": "Abstract. The new sea ice configuration GSI6.0, used in the Met Office global coupled configuration GC2.0, is described and the sea ice extent, thickness and volume are compared with the previous configuration and with observationally based data sets. In the Arctic, the sea ice is thicker in all seasons than in the previous configuration, and there is now better agreement of the modelled concentration and extent with the HadISST data set. In the Antarctic, a warm bias in the ocean model has been exacerbated at the higher resolution of GC2.0, leading to a large reduction in ice extent and volume; further work is required to rectify this in future configurations.",
    "field": "Sea ice modeling",
    "problem": "The previous Met Office sea ice model configuration (GSI4.0) inadequately simulated Arctic sea ice, underestimating its thickness and volume and showing poor agreement with observational data sets for concentration and extent.",
    "method": "This paper describes the development of the Global Sea Ice configuration GSI6.0 by adjusting physical parameters and increasing ocean-ice model resolution, then evaluates its impact on simulated sea ice extent and volume against the previous version and observational data to improve the global coupled model.",
    "task": "The task is to describe and evaluate the new Global Sea Ice 6.0 (GSI6.0) configuration by comparing its simulated sea ice extent, thickness, and volume with the previous configuration and observational data sets.",
